---
/**
 * How it works — the public explanation of the data model:
 * Recommendations → registers → schema'd items; issues → mixed messages;
 * register messages are actions (insert/remove/change); the git patch
 * log is the SSOT, replayed into current and point-in-time editions;
 * full files and snapshots are projections; the two validation proofs.
 */
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const patch1170 = `type: ADD
date_requested: &1 2018-12-15
date_active: *1
ob_issue_no: '1170'
identifier:
  code: 1170-001
data:
  _class: E212MNCAmendment
  entries:
  - country_or_area: {en: Hungary SUP}      # a printed section heading
  - mcc_mnc_codes: 216 04
    networks: Invitech ICT Services Ltd.    # the item being inserted
  # … Kenya SUP, 639 08 Sema Mobile Services Limited …
  notes:
  - "*MCC:  Mobile Country Code / Indicatif de pays du mobile …"`;
---
<Base
  title="How it works"
  masthead
  description="How the ITU Operational Bulletin's printed changes become a replayable record over the international telecommunication registers."
>
  <article class="hw">
    <header class="hw-head">
      <p class="hw-eyebrow">The system</p>
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p class="hw-lede">
        Every change the ITU Operational Bulletin prints for a register is kept
        as a small, typed action — and those actions are replayed to build the
        registers you can search on this site. This page follows one real
        action from the printed page to today's data: the addition of mobile
        network code <span class="num font-mono">216 04</span>.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* Figure: the life of an item */}
    <figure class="hw-life" aria-label="The life of an item, in four steps">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">1 · Printed</span>
          <div class="hw-art">
            <p class="hw-print">Hungary — SUP</p>
            <p class="hw-print"><span class="num font-mono">216 04</span> Invitech ICT Services Ltd.</p>
            <p class="hw-cap">OB № 1170, 15.IV.2019</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">2 · Patched</span>
          <div class="hw-art hw-art-code">
            <pre class="num font-mono">type: ADD
ob_issue_no: '1170'</pre>
            <p class="hw-cap">one file, one action</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">3 · Replayed</span>
          <div class="hw-art">
            <p class="hw-print num font-mono">seed + actions → state</p>
            <p class="hw-cap">the register as it stood at issue № 1170</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">4 · Kept</span>
          <div class="hw-art">
            <p class="hw-print"><span class="num font-mono">216 04</span> Invitech ICT Services Ltd.</p>
            <p class="hw-cap">live today, with its full history</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2 id="registers"><span class="hw-no num">1</span> Recommendations define registers; registers hold items</h2>
      <p>
        ITU-T Recommendations are the normative source. One Recommendation can
        define one or more registers: E.164 alone defines three (the country
        code list, the assignments list, and the dial procedures), and Q.708
        defines two (ISPC and SANC). Seventeen registers are carried here in
        total.
      </p>
      <p>
        A register is a set of <em>items</em> — one charge card issuer, one
        mobile network code, one signalling point. The items of a register all
        share one shape, called its <dfn>data schema</dfn>, and an item is
        made addressable by the register's key: the issuer identifier number
        for E.118, the MCC/MNC pair for E.212, the DNIC for X.121. Addressable
        items are what make change possible to record.
      </p>
      <div class="hw-item hw-art">
        <p class="hw-cap">An item of the E.212 register, as it stands today</p>
        <p class="num font-mono">216 04 — Invitech ICT Services Ltd.</p>
        <p class="hw-cap">key: mcc_mnc_codes · <a href="/registers/e212-mnc/entries/216%2004/">its own page</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="issues"><span class="hw-no num">2</span> An issue is a set of messages; a register message is an action</h2>
      <p>
        Each issue of the Bulletin carries messages of many kinds — running
        annexes, approved Recommendations, service restrictions, telephone
        service, and the register amendments. The amendments are the ones that
        change data, and each of them is an <dfn>action</dfn> on exactly one
        register: it inserts an item, removes an item, or changes an item,
        named by the register's key.
      </p>
      <table class="hw-kinds">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Action</th><th>Means</th><th>Carries</th><th>A real one</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="hw-chip hw-chip-add num font-mono">ADD</span></td>
            <td>insert item</td>
            <td>the complete new item</td>
            <td><a href="/issues/1170/">216 04, issue № 1170</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="hw-chip hw-chip-sup num font-mono">SUP</span></td>
            <td>remove item</td>
            <td>the key</td>
            <td><a href="/registers/e118-iin/entries/89%20351%2007/">89 351 07, issue № 1162</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="hw-chip hw-chip-mod num font-mono">REP</span></td>
            <td>change item</td>
            <td>the key and the new values</td>
            <td>the rarest kind — <a href="/registers/x121-dnic/entries/440%201/">440 1, issue № 1129</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Two further kinds round out the vocabulary: <span class="num font-mono">LIR</span>
        re-prints a row the Bulletin carries again for the reader's
        convenience, and <span class="num font-mono">SEED</span> establishes a
        register's initial full list — the only kind whose payload is a whole
        item set, because an initial state has nothing before it to differ
        from.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="patch"><span class="hw-no num">3</span> The patch log is the record</h2>
      <p>
        Action 1170-001 lives as a small file in the
        <a href="https://github.com/ituob/itu-ob-data" rel="external">patch repository</a>,
        one file per action, reviewed through pull requests like any other
        code. This is the whole patch, lightly abridged:
      </p>
      <figure class="hw-patch">
        <pre class="num font-mono">{patch1170}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          <dfn>type</dfn> the action kind · <dfn>ob_issue_no</dfn> the issue that printed it ·
          <dfn>identifier</dfn> its printed position · <dfn>entries</dfn> the items ·
          <dfn>notes</dfn> the printed footnotes, kept verbatim
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        These patch files are the single source of truth. Patches can always
        regenerate the full list of a register — but a full list cannot be
        turned back into patches, so the full files on this site are
        <em>projections</em> of the log: produced by the build pipeline, never
        edited by hand, and never the input that produces new changes.
        Corrections to published data are new actions, never rewrites.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="replay"><span class="hw-no num">4</span> Replay: any date, on demand</h2>
      <p>
        A register's state is computed, not stored: take the seed (the initial
        full list), apply the actions in issue order, and you have the register
        as it stood after any issue. That is why every register page carries
        its current edition <em>and</em> every past edition.
      </p>
      <p>
        The E.212 seed was set at issue № 1162, and 162 issues have touched
        that register since. The E.118 register holds <span class="num">940</span>
        items today, rebuilt from <span class="num">355</span> recorded changes
        across <span class="num">123</span> issues.
        <a href="/registers/e118-iin/at/1170/">See an edition from 1170</a>, or
        read <a href="/registers/e118-iin/history/">a register's whole change
        history</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="projections"><span class="hw-no num">5</span> The files you see are projections</h2>
      <p>
        The full lists the site renders, the point-in-time snapshots behind the
        edition pages, and the per-register dataset files are all regenerated
        from the patch log by the build pipeline. Each states its provenance —
        which issue it reflects — and none of them is edited directly. When a
        projection and a fresh replay disagree, that is a bug in the pipeline,
        not a judgement call.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="proofs"><span class="hw-no num">6</span> Two proofs, run continuously</h2>
      <p>
        Every item validates against its register's <strong>data schema</strong>,
        and every action validates against the <strong>message schemas</strong> —
        both declared in a single ontology
        (<span class="num font-mono">messages.lml</span>, 86 classes) and checked
        on every build: <span class="num">16,834</span> corpus files at last
        count, with unknown fields rejected rather than ignored. Beyond the
        schemas, the replays themselves are verified: rebuilt snapshots must
        match a fresh replay, and the parsers are checked equivalent against
        captured baselines. Content drift fails the build.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="repos"><span class="hw-no num">7</span> Where everything lives</h2>
      <table class="hw-repos">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ituob/itu-ob-data" rel="external">itu-ob-data</a></th>
            <td>the patch log — every action ever printed, as reviewed pull requests</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ituob/service-publications-docs" rel="external">service-publications-docs</a></th>
            <td>the parsers, the replay engine, the schema ontology, and the proof gates</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ituob/ituob.github.io" rel="external">ituob.github.io</a></th>
            <td>this site — built statically from the replayed registers and served by a Worker</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="hw-end">
        The best way to check any of this is to pick a line in any register and
        follow it: <a href="/registers/">start from the registers</a>, open an
        item, and read its change history back to the issue that printed it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

